19:A criterion may carry the pieces of its rule its own line leaves as an indented bullet sub-list under that line. A bullet holds an enumeration of members, a scope note, or a permitted exception — the material that would otherwise weld a second clause onto the line. Each bullet carries one complete clause with its own subject and finite verb, and carries no code anchor: the anchor belongs at the criterion line's end, and a bracket code inside a bullet is a defect. The sub-list ends at the next criterion, the next case heading, the next requirement, or a blank line followed by unindented text. The bullets belong to their criterion, so every gate that reads a criterion's prose reads them too, each bullet as a sentence of its own.
72:- the style lint — sentence length, no all-capital words outside code anchors, no contrast-by-denial frames, no grading adjectives;

